<!-- Bounded multi-row analysis user for the platform read path (query adapter).

WHY A SECOND USER EXISTS. `lo-query.xml` beside this file was written for one consumer: the Sigma
runner, which asks one aggregate question per window and whose profile therefore states
`max_result_rows = 1`. Everything the platform wants next asks a different question: an RCA bundle
wants the log lines around a finding (200), the anomaly producer wants a series (2 000), an error
budget wants spans (100). Those are the reads `local_observe/store` runs today, and they arrive as
`max_result_rows` = the kind's bound plus one, sent by the client itself.

Which exposes the thing this file exists to fix. `lo-query`'s profile marks `max_result_rows`
`changeable_in_readonly`, so a client that states a bigger number is not refused: the aggregate-only
ceiling that sentence in `lo-query.xml` advertises has been a default, not a bound, ever since the
facade started asking for pages. One credential cannot be both "the user that may read one row" and
"the user that may read two thousand" without the profile meaning nothing, so the two shapes get two
identities and each profile can then state a real number:

  lo-query   the Sigma runner's aggregate path. One row. Its `changeable_in_readonly` entry for
             `max_result_rows` is what lets the facade widen it, and tightening it (deleting that
             one entry) is the reviewer's two-step item recorded in `CONTRACT.md` here: point the
             facade at `lo-read` first, then narrow `lo-query`. Doing it the other way round turns
             every facade read into a settings error that looks like a store outage.
  lo-read    this file. The analysis path: the same seven bounds, a row ceiling stated at the size
             the read model actually uses, and the same three SELECT grants. It adds no table, no
             database and no write.

WHAT THE USER MAY NOT DO, and which setting enforces it (the same three that `lo-query.xml` names,
quoted from the same pages):
  readonly = 1      "Only read data queries are allowed" plus "the user can't change readonly and
                    allow_ddl settings in the current session"
                    (docs/concepts/features/configuration/settings/permissions-for-queries).
                    An INSERT, OPTIMIZE, CREATE, ALTER, DROP or TRUNCATE is refused.
  allow_ddl = 0     allow_ddl is a separate setting with default 1, so readonly alone would still
                    permit DDL. Both are set.
  grants            SELECT on the three SigNoz signal databases only. No system tables, no other
                    database, no column-level grant: "GRANT SELECT ON db.* ... allows john to
                    execute the SELECT query over all the tables in db database"
                    (docs/reference/statements/grant).

THE ROW CEILING, stated where it can be checked. 2 000 is not a taste number: it is
`local_observe/store/client.py:MAX_ROWS`, the transport's own bound, which is also what
`SERIES_MAX_POINTS` and `sigma_runner`'s series reads already carry. The arithmetic is in
`CONTRACT.md` and it is not flattering: the client states `bound + 1` per request (2 001 on the
widest kind), this profile's number is what applies to a request that states nothing, and the entry
below is therefore marked changeable_in_readonly like its sibling's, which means this line is a
declared default and a reviewable number rather than an enforced maximum. A hard maximum would need
a constraints max of 2 001 or higher, and setting one that is lower than what the client sends
refuses the read outright. Both choices are recorded as open in `conformance.md`; neither is a
worker's decision, because each changes what the profile guarantees.

THE PASSWORD IS NOT IN THIS FILE, and it is not the one `lo-query` uses. It is substituted at config
load from a second file, named by the `include_from` below and mounted from the operator's own
private path:
  "If an element has the attribute incl, its value will be replaced by the content of the referenced
   file. The path to the file with substitutions is set by the include_from element in the server
   config ... If a substitution specified in incl does not exist, it is recorded in the log."
   (Same page `lo-query.xml` quotes, section "Substitution with file content".)
Two files, not one: an unresolvable `incl` leaves the element empty, and an empty password element
is a user with no password. Keeping the two users' values in two files means a file written for
`lo-query` cannot silently boot an unpassworded `lo-read` beside it, which is the failure that would
happen if both read the same substitution document and someone forgot the second element. The
Compose delta that mounts this file makes that path a required variable, so the failure lands in
front of the operator at `config` time instead.

Status: reviewed shape, never executed, exactly like its sibling. There is no Docker host in this
environment, so no command in `conformance.md` has been run and nothing here has been observed to
work. It is additionally NOT WIRED: no example includes the delta that mounts it, because the file
that would include it belongs to another item in this wave. Read `CONTRACT.md` ("The second user,
and what it is not yet") before deploying anything that depends on this user existing.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <include_from>/run/secrets/clickhouse-read-credentials</include_from>
    <profiles>
        <lo-analysis>
            <readonly>1</readonly>
            <allow_ddl>0</allow_ddl>
            <!-- The same seven bounds as lo-readonly, at the same sizes, so the analysis path cannot
                 cost the store more time, rows, bytes or memory per query than the Sigma path
                 already costs it. max_memory_usage stays 128 MiB, an eighth of the store's own
                 default profile, so two analysis reads and one Sigma window together still cost the
                 server less than one unbounded UI query. -->
            <max_execution_time>5</max_execution_time>
            <max_rows_to_read>1000000</max_rows_to_read>
            <max_bytes_to_read>67108864</max_bytes_to_read>
            <max_memory_usage>134217728</max_memory_usage>
            <max_result_rows>2000</max_result_rows>
            <!-- The two settings that make truncation a fact rather than a hope: a query that would
                 return more than max_result_rows, or read more than max_rows_to_read, fails instead
                 of returning a short answer. A caller that cannot tell a cut-off page from a whole
                 one is the reason this file is a config fragment and not a comment. -->
            <result_overflow_mode>throw</result_overflow_mode>
            <read_overflow_mode>throw</read_overflow_mode>
            <constraints>
                <max_execution_time><changeable_in_readonly/></max_execution_time>
                <max_rows_to_read><changeable_in_readonly/></max_rows_to_read>
                <max_bytes_to_read><changeable_in_readonly/></max_bytes_to_read>
                <max_memory_usage><changeable_in_readonly/></max_memory_usage>
                <max_result_rows><changeable_in_readonly/></max_result_rows>
                <result_overflow_mode><changeable_in_readonly/></result_overflow_mode>
                <read_overflow_mode><changeable_in_readonly/></read_overflow_mode>
            </constraints>
        </lo-analysis>
    </profiles>
    <users>
        <lo-read>
            <!-- Resolved from /clickhouse/lo_read_password inside the include_from file named above.
                 A plaintext password element is what an X-ClickHouse-Key header over this project's
                 plaintext HTTP network authenticates against, which is the same mechanism lo-query
                 uses and the same reason its value must never appear in this file or in an
                 environment value. -->
            <password incl="lo_read_password"/>
            <profile>lo-analysis</profile>
            <quota>default</quota>
            <!-- No address restriction, for the same reason lo-query has none: the reader is a
                 container on the Compose network whose address is not stable, and the boundary is
                 that network plus this password. Narrow the two entries to the project subnet if you
                 can name it; lo-read's own existence is a reason to want to, because it can read
                 more rows per query than lo-query was ever sized for. -->
            <networks>
                <ip>0.0.0.0/0</ip>
                <ip>::/0</ip>
            </networks>
            <grants>
                <grant>GRANT SELECT ON signoz_traces.*</grant>
                <grant>GRANT SELECT ON signoz_metrics.*</grant>
                <grant>GRANT SELECT ON signoz_logs.*</grant>
            </grants>
        </lo-read>
    </users>
</clickhouse>
